# C/C++ type-bound pointer and reference declarators (local elaboration)

This is a **local** elaboration of shared **c-cpp-standards**. It is not yet in the upstream rulesbase.


## Rule

The pointer declarator `*` and the C++ reference declarators `&` and `&&` must be **applied locally to the type** — written on the **right-hand side of the type** to which they apply, **not** on the left-hand side of the name.

* Glue the operator to the type (no space before `*` / `&` / `&&`); put a space before the identifier (or before a further cv-qualifier on the pointer itself):
  * `T* p`;
  * `T** const* p`;
  * `const T* p`;
  * `T* const p`;
  * `T& r` (C++);
  * `T&& rr` (C++);
* Do **not** place the operator beside the name: forbid `T *p`, `T &r`, `T &&rr`, `const T *p`.
* Prefer **one declarator per declaration** so type-bound `*` / `&` / `&&` cannot be misread across a comma list (`T* p, q` declares only `p` as a pointer).
* The same placement applies in parameters, return types, cast targets, and typedefs/using-aliases.


## Examples

```c
/* ✅ required — * binds to the type */
const char* name;
libver_result* result;
char* const fixed;

/* ❌ forbidden — * binds to the name */
const char *name;
libver_result *result;
char *const fixed;
```

```cpp
/* ✅ required — references bind to the type */
int& ref;
int&& rref;
void f(const std::string& s);

/* ❌ forbidden */
int &ref;
int &&rref;
void f(const std::string &s);
```


## Relationship to west-`const`

* Pointee `const` remains **west-`const`** (`const T* p`, not `T const* p`); see **local-c-cpp-west-const**;
* This rule governs `*` / `&` / `&&` placement relative to the type; west-`const` governs `const` placement on the pointee;


## Scope

* Applies to new and edited C/C++ code in this project.
* When upstream **c-cpp-standards** absorbs this rule, this local file may be removed after sync.
